NOTICE — 橘子岛 (Orange Island)
================================

This product, "橘子岛 / Orange Island", includes software originally developed
by **newo-ether** in the project **Agora**
(https://github.com/newo-ether/Agora) and distributed under the MIT License.

Modifications made in this fork include, but are not limited to:
  - Renaming the application and all related identifiers (package, classes,
    native libraries, resources, themes) from "Agora" to "橘子岛 / Orange Island".
  - Rebranding the user-facing strings, documentation, and metadata.
  - Replacing upstream telemetry/update endpoints with placeholders.
  - Workflow engine, visual editor, and automation subsystem (stage C–E):
    an independent node-and-edge graph executor with trigger integration
    (manual, schedule, intent, app-open, voice, API), live canvas, and
    export/import support. Design and implementation are original to this
    fork and not derived from upstream.
  - Workflow v2 — AI-authored linear workflows (stage F1–F6): a trigger →
    conditions → actions model that an LLM generates, a strict parser, a
    cooldown / daily-cap / condition-gated execution engine, a signal-source
    host covering 19 trigger kinds (manual, schedule, WiFi/power/
    headphones/screen/battery/Bluetooth broadcasts, geofence via Play
    Services GeofencingClient, app-foreground via accessibility events,
    notification-listener, boot), an AI-authoring tool surface gated by a
    user-approval card, and an AI-first UI (read-only detail card, list with
    trigger-summary badges, "How do workflows work?" onboarding).
    The general automation concept (a trigger taxonomy, AI generation of a
    linear definition, a user-approval step) is a broad idea present in many
    automation products. The concrete architecture and all code in this fork
    — the per-signal-source design (each source owns its own Flow
    subscription, no shared registry/diff loop, no per-family callback
    interface), the WorkManager-based durable fire paths for boot/time/
    geofence, the requestId-encoding scheme, the CompletableDeferred approval
    gate, the package-indexed notification filter, every class/field/file
    name — are original and written from scratch. No source code, class
    structure, or file layout was copied from or derived from any
    third-party project, including but not limited to the open-source
    projects whose general automation ideas were studied during design.

